WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.676
This chapter separates the two kinds of sleep apnea.

2
00:00:04.676 --> 00:00:11.904
We start with the core difference, effort versus no effort, and the mechanism behind each.

3
00:00:11.904 --> 00:00:18.008
Then the risk factors and symptoms, which point you toward one or the other.

4
00:00:18.008 --> 00:00:27.003
Next comes diagnosis with polysomnography, how to calculate the apnea hypopnea index, and how to grade severity.

5
00:00:27.003 --> 00:00:39.050
We finish with treatment, which starts very differently: behavior and CPAP for obstructive apnea, and treating the underlying cause for central apnea.

6
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:48.489
The deck's one-line rule: effort but no airflow is obstructive; no effort and no airflow is central.

7
00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:53.222
An apnea that starts central and ends obstructive is mixed.

8
00:00:53.222 --> 00:01:03.169
In obstructive sleep apnea, the upper airway collapses again and again during sleep, and airflow is absent or nearly absent.

9
00:01:03.169 --> 00:01:13.598
But ventilatory effort persists, and on the sleep study the esophageal pressure becomes more negative as the patient works harder.

10
00:01:13.598 --> 00:01:19.454
In central sleep apnea, both airflow and ventilatory effort stop or fall.

11
00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:31.167
The airway is open, but the brain does not send the signal to breathe, so there is no thoraco-abdominal movement and no esophageal pressure swing.

12
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:36.720
In obstructive apnea, sleep reduces muscle tone.

13
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:45.458
The genioglossus fails to oppose the collapsing forces, so the tongue moves back into the oropharynx.

14
00:01:45.458 --> 00:01:52.206
Hess adds that the narrowing is worst in REM sleep and in the supine position.

15
00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:55.148
Central apnea comes in two groups.

16
00:01:55.148 --> 00:02:07.433
Hyperventilation related central apnea is the most common: primary central apnea, Cheyne-Stokes breathing in heart failure, and high altitude.

17
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:21.103
Hypoventilation related central apnea comes from central nervous system disorders, opioids, alcohol or benzodiazepines, neuromuscular disease and severe COPD.

18
00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:32.437
Mixed apnea is an event, not a separate condition, and patients with mostly mixed apneas are classified and treated as obstructive.

19
00:02:32.437 --> 00:02:44.117
Hess adds that central apnea is uncommon, usually occurs with cardiac or neurologic problems, and these patients are usually not obese.

20
00:02:45.533 --> 00:02:57.595
For obstructive apnea, the notes call obesity the strongest predictor, and Hess adds that most patients are obese, with a body mass index above thirty.

21
00:02:57.595 --> 00:03:16.126
Other risk factors are a large neck, over seventeen inches in men and over sixteen in women, male sex, age over sixty five, hypertension, diabetes, chronic nasal congestion, family history, alcohol, sedatives, smoking and ethnicity.

22
00:03:16.126 --> 00:03:29.945
The symptoms are loud snoring, witnessed apneas, abrupt awakenings short of breath, morning headache, dry mouth, excessive daytime sleepiness, and changes in mood or memory.

23
00:03:29.945 --> 00:03:42.646
Central apnea's main causes are heart failure with Cheyne-Stokes breathing, central nervous system disorders or injury, drugs or substances, and high altitude.

24
00:03:42.646 --> 00:03:50.154
Atrial fibrillation often goes with it, so evaluate for cardiac disease and for brain lesions.

25
00:03:50.154 --> 00:03:57.183
Hess says central apnea gives milder symptoms, with less snoring and daytime sleepiness.

26
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:04.134
A full night, attended, in-lab polysomnogram is the gold standard.

27
00:04:04.134 --> 00:04:13.022
The apnea hypopnea index is the number of apneas plus hypopneas, divided by the total sleep time in hours.

28
00:04:13.022 --> 00:04:21.490
The deck's example: one hundred events over eight hours is twelve point five per hour, which is mild.

29
00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:36.164
Mild is five to fifteen events per hour, moderate is fifteen to thirty, and severe is above thirty, or a saturation below ninety percent for more than twenty percent of sleep.

30
00:04:36.164 --> 00:04:48.405
A hypopnea is airflow reduced by at least thirty percent for at least ten seconds, with at least a three percent drop in saturation or an arousal.

31
00:04:48.405 --> 00:04:55.700
Hess's table uses a four percent desaturation; use the deck's three percent or arousal.

32
00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:19.584
Obstructive sleep apnea is confirmed when the index is fifteen or more per hour in a patient without symptoms, or five or more per hour with symptoms, where the events are apneas, hypopneas or respiratory effort related arousals, and when seventy five percent of the events are obstructive.

33
00:05:19.584 --> 00:05:29.198
Central sleep apnea is diagnosed when the majority of events are central, with no airflow and no thoraco-abdominal movement.

34
00:05:29.198 --> 00:05:33.075
Hess sets that at more than fifty percent central.

35
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.548
Pause and work this out.

36
00:05:36.548 --> 00:05:43.459
A sleep study records one hundred apneas and hypopneas over eight hours of sleep.

37
00:05:43.459 --> 00:05:47.982
During the events, the chest and abdomen keep moving.

38
00:05:47.982 --> 00:05:54.125
What is the index, how severe is it, and is this obstructive or central?

39
00:05:55.033 --> 00:06:01.033
[6-second practice pause]

40
00:06:01.533 --> 00:06:11.167
One hundred divided by eight is twelve point five events per hour, which falls in the mild range of five to fifteen.

41
00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:21.631
The chest and abdomen keep moving, which means ventilatory effort persists while airflow stops, so the events are obstructive.

42
00:06:21.631 --> 00:06:27.445
Central events would show no effort and no thoraco-abdominal movement.

43
00:06:27.445 --> 00:06:38.158
To confirm obstructive sleep apnea at this index, the patient would also need symptoms, since twelve point five is below fifteen.

44
00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:41.636
The first step differs.

45
00:06:41.636 --> 00:06:52.703
For obstructive apnea, start with behavior: weight loss, avoiding alcohol and sedatives, and avoiding sleeping on the back.

46
00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:56.662
Then positive airway pressure is first line.

47
00:06:56.662 --> 00:07:05.120
CPAP is the most common and most effective, because constant pressure splints the airway open.

48
00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:12.048
BiPAP is used for CPAP intolerance or hypoventilation, or auto-titrating PAP.

49
00:07:12.048 --> 00:07:19.786
Other options are oral appliances, surgery and an implantable upper airway stimulator.

50
00:07:19.786 --> 00:07:27.974
For central apnea, treat the underlying cause first, such as heart failure or encephalitis.

51
00:07:27.974 --> 00:07:43.990
If it persists, use CPAP or BiPAP, with an initial CPAP of five to ten centimeters of water, and RCP 203 lists sleep apnea, especially central sleep apnea, as a BiPAP indication.

52
00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:56.767
Respiratory stimulants such as acetazolamide and theophylline are options, and oxygen reduces desaturation but does not improve sleep quality.

53
00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:11.800
RCP 203 says to set CPAP by prescription, or use auto CPAP with a maximum of fifteen centimeters of water, and optimum control is an index below five per hour.

54
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:21.123
If CPAP is not tolerated, try other masks, humidification, a ramp, auto-titration, pressure relief, or BiPAP.

55
00:08:21.123 --> 00:08:36.348
Hess adds that the usual obstructive apnea CPAP is eight to ten centimeters of water, and that the spontaneous timed bilevel mode supplies a backup rate if the patient is apneic.

56
00:08:36.348 --> 00:08:42.079
RCP 203 sets that rate two to five breaths below the patient's own.

57
00:08:42.079 --> 00:08:57.475
Adaptive servo ventilation adjusts the pressure support breath by breath, and the deck says it helps hyperventilation related central apnea, such as Cheyne-Stokes in heart failure.

58
00:08:57.475 --> 00:09:12.700
Know the Hess caveat: ASV in Cheyne-Stokes has been linked to harm, and it should be stopped in symptomatic heart failure with an ejection fraction of forty five percent or less.

59
00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:14.858
One more.

60
00:09:14.858 --> 00:09:21.763
A patient with heart failure has central sleep apnea with a Cheyne-Stokes pattern.

61
00:09:21.763 --> 00:09:28.500
What is the first step, and if the apnea persists, what does the deck recommend?

62
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:35.400
[6-second practice pause]

63
00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:40.560
First, treat the underlying cause, here the heart failure.

64
00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:48.596
If the central apnea persists, use CPAP or BiPAP, starting CPAP at five to ten centimeters of water.

65
00:09:48.596 --> 00:09:59.926
The deck adds that adaptive servo ventilation is particularly helpful for this hyperventilation related type, Cheyne-Stokes in heart failure.

66
00:09:59.926 --> 00:10:14.550
But know the Hess caveat: ASV in Cheyne-Stokes has been linked to harm, and it should be stopped in symptomatic heart failure with an ejection fraction of forty five percent or less.

67
00:10:15.967 --> 00:10:18.466
Here is the chapter in brief.

68
00:10:18.466 --> 00:10:28.461
Effort without airflow is obstructive; no effort and no airflow is central; mixed apneas are treated as obstructive.

69
00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:37.682
Obstructive apnea comes from lost muscle tone and a falling tongue, and obesity is the strongest predictor.

70
00:10:37.682 --> 00:10:42.507
Central apnea comes from the brain, often heart failure.

71
00:10:42.507 --> 00:10:50.177
The index is events divided by sleep hours, with five, fifteen and thirty as the cutoffs.

72
00:10:50.177 --> 00:10:59.742
Obstructive apnea is confirmed at fifteen, or five with symptoms, with seventy five percent obstructive events.

73
00:10:59.742 --> 00:11:07.842
Treat obstructive apnea with behavior and CPAP, and central apnea by treating the cause first.
